Not necessarily a very remarkable ascension, but my only NH 3.4.3 game
so far.

So maybe I should explain the name first.  After my last ascension I had
decided that this game takes too much of my time, and I would wait until
next year (2004) before playing another one.  But then I started a new
one on the same day, so I decided - aptly - to call my character idiot.
Also I deliberately chose a low-intelligence class.  However, I
actually managed not to play her, because I saved the game on Dlvl 1,
maybe even at turn 1 - and forgot it.  The game was started as an NH
3.4.0 one.

This year I finally made up my mind to install the latest nethack
version, and since the instructions said that it would be compatible
with saved games from 3.4.0 onwards, I checked whether I still had
some unfinished, non-explore mode game (I sometimes keep copies of
games at interesting places - essentially on Dlvl 1 before moving on
to the planes and on the Astral Plane, and get back to them after the
ascension; then I switch them to explore mode, because they were made
exactly for that -- exploring :-) ).

Well, I found idiot and decided to give her a try.  (I had to rename the
saved file first to be able to use it.)

The initial game with a ranger is not too difficult, as long as he/she
still has enough arrows.  My major problem was that it took 16708 turns
before I got telepathy.  There were only few floating eyes, and the
first ones either did not leave a corpse or were eaten by my pet.  There
was an armor shop on Dlvl 3, which helped a lot with the initial
survival (dwarvish mithril), and I got lucky with a polymorph trap on
Mines 9, which just changed me into a new orc.  This is the first game,
where I identified the scroll of scare monster without picking it up.
Stepping on a scroll, I suddenly got the message "the ... turns to flee"
or s.th. like that.  So I jotted down: Verr Yed Horre is scare monster.

I almost died at the bottom of tje Mines when I was surrounded by winter
wolf cubs.  Since I had an uncursed scroll of genocide, I tried to get
rid of them by genociding winter wolves.  This inaccuracy almost cost
the life of the character...  In addition, after having finished off the
last one with only 17 HP left, I noticed that I had a ring of cold
resistance in my inventory, which would have helped a lot, since I was
hit by several cold bolts...

Mixed up a loadstone on the bottom of the mines with the luckstone and
had to get rid of it the same way I had gotten rid of cursed (fumble)
boots before - by dipping the cursed item into fountains until it got
uncursed.  At that point I thought having not played for a much too
long period, because it never happened to me before that I foolishly
picked up/put on two cursed items in one game.  With the boots, I had
been sure that my pet had stepped on them before without any message
(such as ... moves only reluctantly).  With the stone, I had seen the 
luckstone before and kicked it, but it did not move (since it was in a 
corner). So I believed the luckstone to be a loadstone and picked up the
loadstone without double-checking...

There were several altars with priests around in the main dungeon, so
buying protection was only limited by the gold found -- I never saw
Ludios.  Nevertheless, I ended up with an intrinsic protection of 10 points.

The main problem of the middle game, when I had an AC of about -15 (and
according to my previous record should not fail to ascend :-) ), was the
lack of magic resistance.  I hit two more polymorph traps.  One was on
the quest and changed me into a jackal.  Luckily, I had already cleared
the level, so there were no monsters engaging me into immediate melee.
Threw a gem to the ceiling, some rocks fell onto my head and made me
return to orcish form.  I then had enough time to put back on all my
armour.  I don't remember what the second trap turned me into (it was
not a large beast, so I lost no armour) but it was clear that I had to
get magic resistance soon.  Unfortunately, the artifact spoiler told me
that the Longbow of Diana does not provide it.

Doing the quest, I had a wand of death already but decided to try a wand 
of sleep on Scorpius first. He fell asleep easily and to save time, I 
used the wand of death nonetheless.  Since there is a throne on the 
quest bottom level, I sat on it: "You may wish for an item..."  Well, 
this was my  magic resistance, a gray dragon scale mail.  By that time I 
believed that only Demogorgon or one of the riders might now bar me from 
ascending.

The first artifact I got by sacrificing was Stormbringer, so it became
difficult to keep my pets alive.  I switched between my +5 elven
sword and Stormbringer.  Medusa was already on Dlvl 21 and the titan was
a pain in the neck, summoning many monsters, including a minotaur that
took my hitpoints away in no time.  So back up to heal and to do some
thinking.

Then I had the solution, which was quite easy.  I put on my ring of
conflict and went back down on the Medusa level.  Also I left my bag of 
holding upstairs (on a square with "Elbereth" engraved), so I was 
unencumbered.  But the minotaur was no problem anymore, being occupied 
hitting his fellow monsters instead of myself.  Also the titan fell to a 
few arrows.  I had both a ring of levitation and water walking boots, so 
all I had to make sure was to kill the eels in the moat.  To this end, 
my collected poisoned arrows were very handy.  A few "the poison was 
deadly..." later, I could pass on to Medusa.  I did not have a mirror 
handy, so she escaped downstairs, but I followed her and killed her 
without any problems.  On Dlvl 22, I ate a leprechaun from one of those 
halls, giving me teleportitis.  But I supposed that the castle was not 
far...

However, first I tamed an Olog-Hai and investigated the levels above 20 
again in the hope of identifying one of the many rings I had as teleport 
control.  Olly killed some of the priests, even when he was not supposed
to...  But I got a robe and a few useful spells from that.

On the castle level, eels were again killed by arrows, the drawbridge
was destroyed with a wand of striking and then ordinary monsters were
attacked using Stormy, whereas lichs and disenchanters were taken out by
arrows or wands.

The wand of wishing had two wishes, one went for two blessed scrolls of
charging and a ring of teleport control, two others for a +2 blessed
rustproof helm of brilliance and a magic marker.  One wish I kept for
emergency cases (I was thinking of wishing for a cockatrice corpse,
should Demogorgon appear).

Lured some wraiths of the Valley of the Dead down to the first level of
Gehennom, where they left a number of corpses, helping me to increase my
level and to become better at spell casting, which however did not turn
out too useful until I could enhance my divination spell capacities, so
I could detect treasures with zero failure rate, which provided another
magic marker.  On the downside was that I had to take off my shield of
reflection to cast spells and that at one point, while I was not
wielding the Longbow, a blue dragon made my ring of levitation explode.
It could have been worse - I had seen plenty of rings of levitation
lying around.  But there was only one ring of conflict in the game and
only a second ring of teleport control (which was cursed, strangely).
Also there was no ring of free action which made me worry a bit.

Gehennom was mostly uneventful.  Juiblex was on Dlvl 30, and I had a
potion of full healing ready when he swallowed me.  After digging out
and killing him by one hit, I quaffed my potion and that was done.
Dlvls 32 and 34 were inhabited by Asmodeus and Baalzebub, who were
easily disposed of.  Ah, I forgot to say that before descending into the
Valley, I had written a blessed scroll of genocide and extinguished all
h.  I did not mind hobbits and dwarves, they just had to go, because I
did not want to waste two uncursed scrolls instead of one blessed one.
No other monsters were genocided, I find L and ; quite manageable.

Orcus was surprisingly tough.  Since I was only at AC -30 at the time,
he reduced me by about 70 Hp down to 130, before he was gone.  In
addition, I had big trouble with shades.  I must have been surrounded by
three of them and I took them down using Stormbringer, not thinking
clearly.  Just for silver haters I had actually picked up a silver saber
earlier...  However, after the shades had reduced my Hp a lot before
being destroyed, I thought about acquiring a silver saber and realized
that I already had one.  I never enchanted it.  At +0, it was still more
efficient against V and & than the +3 Stormy, not to speak of the +5
eleven broadsword.  Vlad was no problem, as usual.

The bottom of the dungeon was already at Dlvl 48.

A visit back to the main dungeon was used to improve my AC down to -48
and to boost Stormy to +6. After having found the second magic marker,
another visit to the main dungeon allowed me to get the Longbow to +6
and I also enchanted the 350+ arrows that I had produced in the meantime
by invoking the Longbow (and using almost always my melee weapons,
except for ; and disenchanters) to +6.

The event that made me lose my ring of levitation happened actually when 
I was already in the bottom level of the Wizard's tower, so I had to 
make a third journey back to the main dungeon to replace the ring.

But then I took on the Wizard.  Since I had only few charges in my two 
wands of death (the second from Orcus), I decided to mostly rely on 
Stormy -- if you are patient enough this has to work, because he keeps 
losing levels, so teleporting away to heal does not help that much...

Invocation, down to the Sanctum.  The Wizard appeared.  This time I used 
a wand of death, being impatient.  He reappeared.  I used Stormy, he 
teleported away, but not to the stairs, rather towards the Sanctum.  I 
observed this behaviour before, but never in NH 3.3 or earlier, so this 
seems to belong to NH 3.4.  Anyway, I ended up causing conflict 
everywhere (I had long been petless, Olly had eaten a chameleon corpse 
and changed into a mumak which did not survive too long).  At one point 
I had to deal with the high priest, two versions of the wizard and an 
arch-lich.  But it was not too bad.  I finally used a death ray on the 
high priest who seemed to hit hardest.  Then there were two Amulets of 
Yendor.  But only one of them did not fit into my sack and this was not 
due to the fact that the sack contained already a bag of holding...  So 
this amulet was named genuine, the other thrown away.

After having left Gehennom, I decided that Demogorgon would not appear 
anymore, so I could use my final wish for another blessed magic marker, 
which wrote mostly scrolls of charging (to recharge my two wands of 
death) and of enchant weapon.  After all, I had a +0 Fire Brand as a 
second gift from Mars, which I now enchanted up to +7 - it would be more 
  efficient on the Astral Plane than Stormbringer.  Also, I like it when 
I get a message like: The potion of full healing of the arch lich boils...
(which happened).  I also prayed a last time (or so I thought) to make a 
bit more holy water.  Moving some items from my bag of holding to the 
sack (for example three uncursed scrolls of gold detection), I was 
prepared for the planes.

By the way, reading an uncursed scroll of teleportation in a confused 
state to do level teleport does not work as before any more.  In the 
spoilers, I read that sometimes you are teleported randomly.  Usually I 
confuse myself using a burnt-out spell.  This has also changed in that 
it stuns you too now (always?).  I experimented a bit and it seems that 
confused teleport was random only, when I did not wait, after having 
used my burnt-out spell, until I was not stun any longer.  When I was 
only confused but not stun, it seemed to work as in the past.  Is this 
right or was it only a coincidence?

All the planes before Astral were easy.  I had already noticed earlier 
that air elementals in the dungeon were pretty wimpy - I never needed a 
wand of death, they fell to a few strikes of Stormy.  On the plane of 
air, I also did not use the wand of death (although I used one of my 
plenty wands of polymorph, just for fun).  Wands of teleportation are 
now enough to bring you safely through the plane of air, because no air 
elemental can pummel you during more than one turn...  Well, to be true, 
I put on my ring of conflict for quite a while to escape the crowd...

The end was easy, too, because the altar guarded by Famine, which I 
always visit first, was the right one.  So when I left the game, I still 
had more than 300 of my carefully collected arrows...  And they are less 
useful than I had thought, because despite the arrows, Famine managed to 
get close to me and make "my body shrivel" once.  With Pestilence, this 
would have been really bad.  But I still have an explore mode copy of 
that part of the game and will use it for training purposes - to visit 
all the altars...

Since I did not know whether praying on the altar of your God on the 
Astral Plane is problematic or not, I just tried it out before 
ascending.  But this was of course inconclusive, as I realized when Mars 
was displeased.  I had prayed shortly before, when preparing to enter 
the planes.  Can anyone of the source divers tell me whether praying 
would work as usual (one might think your God is always pissed off, if 
you pray with the Amulet in your possession -- out of impatience). 
Anyway, this is why my AC was only -38 at the end.

